WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.087 --> 00:00:01.290
(upbeat music)

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.310
- [Narrator] Meal kit companies

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.140
set out to disrupt grocery stores

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.180
and change the way we cook at home.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.030
Now, there's 382 of them in the US,

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.730
a 300% increase from a decade ago,

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.860
when they were just 13.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.510
Despite industry-wide growth,

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.810
the meal kit model faces an obstacle, you.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.040
Of all the people who tried

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.310
one of five major meal
delivery services in 2022,

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.380
about 90% canceled their subscription

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.490
by the end of the year.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.950
- Human behavior is very fickle,

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.750
especially when it comes
to food and beverage.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.130
- [Narrator] And now, Blue Apron,

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.650
one of the earliest players is selling.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.323
This is The Economics of Meal Kits.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.090
This graph shows the
share of sales in the US

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.330
between the major meal
kit companies in 2022

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:49.020
Sunbasket and Marley
Spoon Inc., have 2 and 3%,

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.140
Blue Apron and Home Chef took 6 and 12%

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.520
while HelloFresh and its
subsidiaries ate up 78%.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.960
But no matter their size,

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.540
many of these companies use
the same key ingredients

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.340
to find and keep customers,

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.460
price point, convenience, and variety.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.680
First, get the customer.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.680
Meal kit companies focus a
lot on the price per meal.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.180
- [Presenter 1] Here's
how I make fancy meals

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:13.830
for under $5.
- [Presenter 2] Wow!

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.570
- [Presenter 3] Get 1.49 per meal.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.150
- Our biggest competitor
are the offline grocers.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.310
- [Narrator] Which is why
HelloFresh and Home Chef

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.700
say, "It's cheaper to buy meal
kits than to buy groceries.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.830
- Wherever you source the ingredients

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:28.020
to do home cooking from scratch yourself,

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.480
that's our competition in some way.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.160
- [Narrator] But what makes that possible?

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.870
- It's all about economies of scale.

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.850
- [Narrator] Brian Choi
has done market research

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.670
on the food and beverage
industry for 15 years,

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:39.577
and he says that,

00:01:39.577 --> 00:01:42.240
"When it comes to keeping
prices low for the consumer,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.890
grocery stores have an advantage

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.420
over most meal kit companies

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.360
because of the volume of
product they handle daily.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.310
But similarly, the meal kit companies

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.510
that are best positioned

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.640
to offer the lowest price
point for consumers,

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:56.100
are the biggest ones, like HelloFresh."

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.670
- Economies of scale allow
us to get better pricing,

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:02.070
to get a better margin on the product,

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.350
and then to reinvest parts of that margin,

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.170
either into a better customer experience

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:08.700
or into lower pricing.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.140
- [Narrator] Which means that at times,

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.840
the company can compete
on price with grocers,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.430
but on a per-meal basis,

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.860
buying the ingredients
yourself from a grocery store

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.650
still tends to be cheaper,
especially considering

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.870
that you can always buy fewer ingredients

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.550
or go for cheaper options.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.980
Whereas the minimum you
could spend on HelloFresh

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.397
and Blue Apron in a week is $60.95,

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:34.290
$12.49 per meal, at
least before discounts.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.120
- It's sometimes hard to get people

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.590
over the purchase barrier.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.750
That's why incentives are a key part

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.510
of our marketing or growth playbook.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.430
For every dollar that we spend

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.590
on getting that group of customers,

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.120
it takes about six months

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.720
to actually earn that dollar
that we invested back.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:52.950
- [Narrator] Which makes the next step

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:56.160
all the more important,
keeping the customer.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.110
These are the customer retention rates

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.200
for the same five meal
kit companies in 2022.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.520
In less than a year,

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.460
they all lost the vast
majority of the new customers

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.130
that bought their first
meal kits in January.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:11.550
One possible reason everyone
drops off, the discounts.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.650
- They're doing their own mental calculus.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:17.190
They're like, "Wow, $12 meal
is very different from the $4,

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.380
you know, based on the promotional rate."

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.210
- [Narrator] When you
introduce a product to someone

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:22.500
at a severe discount,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.240
you may get more people to try it,

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:25.680
but when the discount goes away

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.300
and they have to pay full price,

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.550
it may not be worth
that new price to them.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.170
- One thing about the American consumer

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.860
is they don't like
dramatic change in prices.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.950
- [Narrator] So how do companies
get retention rates up?

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.320
One way is to offer the discounts again,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.990
and hope that they entice
customers to return.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:46.050
And another is to add more
convenience and variety.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.380
- The biggest value
proposition is the convenience.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:51.390
- [Narrator] The big thing
meal kits offer to consumers

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.400
is a convenient way to cook at home.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:54.450
They ship to your door

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:56.190
and give you pre-portioned ingredients

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.080
for relatively simple recipes.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.270
- But when you stack that versus

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.670
the other options that
are there for consumers,

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:04.320
the collective value proposition

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.390
has diminished significantly.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:07.920
- [Narrator] With restaurants
and grocery stores

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.630
offering delivery to your door,

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:10.770
it's hard for meal kits

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:13.500
to offer a unique
convenience to customers,

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.240
and getting pre-portioned ingredients

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.940
maybe more convenient if you
want to cook for yourself.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.680
But Americans only cook an average

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.230
of 4.5 meals at home per week.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.330
But companies across the meal kit industry

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.770
are expanding their offerings,

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:28.620
and one of the common ones
isn't a meal kit at all,

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.510
it's just pre-made meals.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:32.640
- [Announcer 1] Factor has
chefs cook meals for you

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:34.470
and then they deliver
them fresh to your house.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:35.400
- [Announcer 2] They have oven-ready meals

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:36.480
with everything included

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:37.530
and fast and fresh meals

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.090
that can be done in 15 minutes.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:40.650
- As you grow your customer base

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:42.060
and you grow your assortment,

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.090
you also tend to give
customers a lot more choice.

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:45.990
- [Narrator] And more choice

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:47.850
means you're more likely to get customers

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:49.500
to spend more with your company,

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:50.640
but it's yet to be seen

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.610
how much these new offerings
will increase retention rates

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:55.290
across meal kit companies,

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:57.990
plus retention rates aren't everything.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:04:59.190
Looking back at this chart,

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.500
Blue Apron sits slightly above the rest,

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.240
which Choi says, "May be because

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:06.240
it offers slightly more
options and customization."

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.110
But Blue Aprons' revenue has
declined greatly since 2017,

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:11.700
then, there's profit,

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:13.410
Both HelloFresh and Blue Apron

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:14.340
got off the ground

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.250
by raising hundreds of
millions of dollars in funding,

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.770
in 2017, they both went public,

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:22.290
then their paths split.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.630
Blue Apron's revenue has slowly declined

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:26.070
with just a slight increase

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.080
during the industry's pandemic boom.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:29.310
HelloFresh on the other hand,

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:32.220
has been consistently
profitable since 2017.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.920
Looking at its revenue,
you see constant growth,

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.050
even after the pandemic shutdowns ended

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:38.970
and people could eat at restaurants again.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:42.600
- Once you establish yourself
as the leader in that space,

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.630
it becomes very, very hard to be disrupted

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:48.510
because you need to solve
a lot of complex problems

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:51.720
over and over again to
get to that position.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.340
- [Narrator] While both faced stark drops

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:54.510
in their share prices,

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:57.420
the New York Stock Exchange
threatened to delist Blue Apron

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:59.610
after its stock dipped below a dollar,

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.910
the company shifted to an
asset-light model, scaling down,

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:05.970
and offloading many of its
operations to another company.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:08.310
Then it announced its
selling to Wonder Group,

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:09.660
a food delivery startup,

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:12.000
and return to being a private company.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.430
Another key step is to differentiate.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:17.820
The barriers to enter the meal
kit industry are fairly low,

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:18.870
which is partly why

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:21.360
there are so many meal
kit companies today.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:22.890
- But the barriers to scale

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.620
and build a big business
are actually really high

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:26.670
- [Narrator] And why it's important

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:29.670
for each one to stand out
if it wants to survive.

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:32.220
Take Methodology, a small
meal delivery company

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.020
that started in 2015,

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:36.210
it doesn't offer meal kits at all,

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:37.980
instead, Methodology sends out

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.200
four or five days' worth of pre-made meals

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.140
with ingredients you're less
likely to find in other kits,

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:45.510
like nopales, cacti, and purslane.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:46.890
- Our audience is basically

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:48.840
who I was when I started the business.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.250
They're really time-starved,

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:53.490
so they don't have time to cook
healthy meals on weeknights,

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:55.770
and as far as their incomes,

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.050
most are making in the
several hundred thousands,

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.430
if not more than that.

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:01.710
- [Narrator] Most other companies
compete at a price point

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:05.670
around $8 to $15 per
serving without discounts.

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:09.690
But Methodology costs
around 17 to $30 per meal.

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:11.190
- Our sweet spot actually

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:13.290
is men who live on takeout

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:14.340
'cause they're already doing it

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:15.780
three to four times a week minimum,

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:17.970
they're spending 35 on average per meal.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:19.290
- [Narrator] And it
doesn't offer discounts

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:21.780
at nearly the same rate
as the other companies.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:23.730
The average discount for a Methodology Kit

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:26.400
is 10% off a customer's first week.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.800
Nguyen says, "This model
has allowed the company

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.780
to be profitable from year two."

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:31.980
- We have customers who have spent

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:33.450
over a hundred thousand dollars with us

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:37.740
because we really are a lifestyle
for our target customer.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:38.910
- We're operating in one of

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:41.340
the largest consumer categories out there,

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:43.050
which is food at home.

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:44.130
So in my view,

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:46.980
a large category will always attract

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:48.960
sort of like new business models,

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.780
new entrants in the category, and so on.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.670
- [Narrator] Grand View
Research forecasts that

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:56.220
the revenue across the
US meal kit industry

00:07:56.220 --> 00:08:00.240
will reach $64.27 billion by 2030.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.710
- Do I believe the numbers?

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:04.590
- I think they're probably
a little bit too optimistic.

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:06.180
- I don't know what the food industry

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:08.400
will look like in five or 10 years,

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:11.790
but I do think that we
can play a really big role

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:14.460
in shaping the future
of the food industry.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:17.040
I think in 10 years, we'll probably see

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:20.280
one or two major players in the industry

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.740
still operating in that space.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:25.680
I'll also expect to see some companies

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:26.970
that have gone bankrupt

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:30.169
or acquired by some of the larger players.

00:08:30.169 --> 00:08:32.919
(rhythmic music)

